WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:11.075
- Well welcome to the Khan
Academy Daily Homeroom.

00:00:11.075 --> 00:00:14.010
This is a way that we're
trying to stay in touch

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:17.480
and help support parents,
teachers, and students

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:20.900
as we go through this
school closure situation.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:22.550
Many of y'all know, at Khan Academy,

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:24.720
we're not for profit with
a mission of providing

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.280
a free world class education
for anyone, anywhere

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:29.460
and we could have never
predicted the scenario

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:32.130
we're now in, this very
suboptimal situation.

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:34.850
But over the last 10 years
or so we've been building

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:38.790
resources that now can
really help you as a student,

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:41.660
a parent, or a teacher bridge, make sure

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:45.200
that everyone can keep
learning through this crisis.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:48.200
I do wanna remind folks
that we are not for profit,

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:50.240
we are funded by philanthropic donations

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:51.780
from folks like yourself.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:54.420
So if you're in a position
to do so, please think about

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.860
donating to Khan Academy,
you can see some links

00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:58.450
on the screen right over here.

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:01.400
I do want do, give a
special shout out to several

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:05.170
help us fund our gap.

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:07.210
We were running at a deficit even before

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:09.900
this crisis started, and now our usage

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.547
is about 250% of normal
and so you can imagine

00:01:12.547 --> 00:01:14.410
our budget situation has gotten

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:15.710
even a little bit more serious.

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:17.736
So special thanks to Bank of America,

00:01:17.736 --> 00:01:19.610
which is really the first to step up,

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:21.240
that first weekend when
the school closures

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:23.340
were happening, we were
seeing that traffic spike,

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:26.740
followed closely by AT&amp;T,
Google.org and Novartis.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:29.110
So whether you're an individual or someone

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:31.350
who represents a corporation,
we still need help,

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:33.360
we're still running at a
deficit, and we have a lot

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:34.930
of ideas of what we would like to do

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:37.040
to support more folks through this crisis.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:42.040
But we are somewhat gated
by resources at the moment.

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:46.140
Now, the fun of this
session is it's really,

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:48.190
we wanna make it as
interactive as possible.

00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:50.610
So whatever social media
you're watching this on,

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:53.400
whether it's Facebook or
YouTube or someplace else,

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:55.441
that we're always adding new things to it,

00:01:55.441 --> 00:01:59.050
feel free, ask questions
on the discussion board.

00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:03.960
And this is a forum, very
informal, literally ask anything.

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:05.650
I would like to be surprised.

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:08.310
No question will offend us and it's fun

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:12.890
to discuss not just how Khan
Academy resources might be used

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:14.810
or how we might navigate this crisis

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:17.110
or implications in
education, these are all

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:19.930
very interesting topics,
but I'm also happy to answer

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:23.990
questions about other things
that are interesting to you

00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:25.870
or maybe sometimes we
wanna talk about things

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:28.220
other than COVID right
now, but we can obviously

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:29.350
talk about that as well.

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:31.950
And I have team members
on Khan Academy, Dan,

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:34.708
who will join us and there's
Dan right over there,

00:02:34.708 --> 00:02:38.150
who will help, myself and Dan,
and some of our team members

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:40.180
are gonna be looking at the
comments as they come in.

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:44.540
And so we will answer any
questions that you might have.

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:49.380
And so if I look, and we
have a few announcements

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:51.750
that we've been making over
the last couple of days.

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:56.580
One of them is we've
released these schedules

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:58.750
about three, four weeks
ago, that first weekend

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:00.720
when the closures were
happening and we get

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.770
a lot of feedback from
parents, students, and teachers

00:03:03.770 --> 00:03:06.570
that they would want even
more detailed resources,

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:08.520
so above and beyond the
live stream, the schedules,

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:11.370
the webinars we're running,
we released learning plans

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:14.830
for students to keep them
learning through the summer.

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:17.020
Not just through the
end of the school year,

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:19.400
but also leveraging summer
for a time of learning.

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:22.410
And so these are, if you
to our main, I guess,

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:26.087
COVID Response Center, you can
see the URLs on the screen,

00:03:26.087 --> 00:03:28.370
you will see these new learning plans.

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:30.250
And depending on whether your
third grade, fourth grade,

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:32.594
fifth grade, all the
way up through algebra

00:03:32.594 --> 00:03:37.190
and algebra two and geometry,
a week by week goals

00:03:37.190 --> 00:03:39.560
for getting mastery on Khan Academy.

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:41.570
So highly encourage
folks to check that out

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:44.110
and keep learning over break.

00:03:44.110 --> 00:03:47.620
And so let's see, we have,
we're starting to get

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:52.620
some questions, one from
YouTube, Anouj Baguan says,

00:03:53.367 --> 00:03:58.367
"Hey Sal, what motivates you
to always push the limits?"

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:02.724
Well, I don't know if I'm
always pushing the limits,

00:04:02.724 --> 00:04:07.480
but I think the things that
Khan Academy is working on

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:09.986
and our mission and what we aspire to do,

00:04:09.986 --> 00:04:11.770
there's urgency to it.

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:14.470
You have, even before this COVID crisis,

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:17.500
you have hundreds of millions
of students around the world,

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:21.090
arguably billions, who
have access to some form

00:04:21.090 --> 00:04:22.870
of schooling but could use more support.

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:25.410
So you have millions of
teachers around the world

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:28.300
who know that they wanna
support their students

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:30.470
in better ways, but it's
difficult if you're a teacher

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:32.780
in a classroom of 30
students and every student

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:34.700
has their own different learning needs,

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:36.090
is learning at a different pace.

00:04:36.090 --> 00:04:38.250
They need tools to be
able to support them.

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:41.044
And all of these things
of how do you allow

00:04:41.044 --> 00:04:43.769
more people to have access
to world class resources,

00:04:43.769 --> 00:04:46.329
how do you create better
tools for teachers,

00:04:46.329 --> 00:04:49.160
how do you connect that
to opportunity that exists

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:52.810
in the real world and higher
education or internships

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:55.280
or jobs, those are always important

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:57.520
and they've just now
become a lot more important

00:04:57.520 --> 00:04:58.820
because of this COVID crisis.

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:01.680
So I speak for myself and I
speak for a lot of the team

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:03.680
members and a lot of the
folks supporting Khan Academy,

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:05.086
there's an urgency here.

00:05:05.086 --> 00:05:08.760
I always think about for
every Albert Einstein

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:11.920
we know about or every
Marie Curie we know about,

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:15.890
how many others we're never
able to fulfill their potential.

00:05:15.890 --> 00:05:19.160
And that's a shame even
on individual level

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:21.450
that they could have
maybe done so much more

00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:23.850
and it's also a shame for
humanity because that person

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:27.050
could have found the cure for
a disease or could have found

00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:29.870
a way to solve a major
issue in our society.

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:33.420
And I suspect that for every
Albert or Alberta Einstein

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:36.420
we discover, historically
there's probably been

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:39.560
50 or 100 that we don't
discover, that never got

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:41.640
the educational opportunities,
never got to tap

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:42.480
into their potential.

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:45.640
And so Khan Academy can
be part of the solution

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:49.000
to increase by an order of
magnitude, by a factor of 10

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:50.820
the number that we can discover.

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:54.240
And not just those very
special circumstances

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:56.520
like an Albert Einstein,
but the whole distribution.

00:05:56.520 --> 00:05:59.070
So more people can
participate in the economy.

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:02.090
We're definitely getting
into a world with automation

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:07.070
that you need higher level
skills in order to participate.

00:06:07.070 --> 00:06:10.265
We know what's happening with
robotics, with computing,

00:06:10.265 --> 00:06:12.680
that's going to be able
to do a lot of the skills

00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:14.900
that labor has historically done.

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:18.790
So as we get more productive,
we have two choices.

00:06:18.790 --> 00:06:20.550
We either have to get all
of humanity to be able

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:22.400
to participate in the knowledge economy,

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:24.340
and I hope Khan Academy
can play a role there,

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:26.190
or we're going to have tougher times.

00:06:26.190 --> 00:06:27.881
And obviously what's
going on economically now

00:06:27.881 --> 00:06:30.410
makes that even clearer.

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:33.730
So let's see, other
questions that I'm getting,

00:06:33.730 --> 00:06:36.850
and I'm having my regular
mouse is a little strange

00:06:36.850 --> 00:06:39.930
right now so please bear
with me a little bit.

00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:44.727
So there is Facebook, Neon Khan asks,

00:06:44.727 --> 00:06:47.797
"Sal, can you speak
Bengali for me, please."

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:51.531
Any of my Bengali family
members will cringe

00:06:51.531 --> 00:06:53.375
if I start to speak Bengali.

00:06:53.375 --> 00:06:55.530
(speaking foreign language).

00:06:55.530 --> 00:06:57.337
Our accent (speaking foreign language).

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:06.040
So for those of you who
do not know Bengali,

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:08.400
I just said I can speak Bengali,

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:11.600
I can get by, but my accent is really bad.

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:13.983
But I try.

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:19.070
I'm sorry, I think I
made about 400 million

00:07:19.070 --> 00:07:21.580
Bengali speakers around the world cringe.

00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:23.233
But that's, I guess, you know.

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:26.560
Neon asked for that.

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:31.347
All right, so from YouTube,
Katherine Pierce asks,

00:07:31.347 --> 00:07:34.430
"What's coming to Khan Academy next?"

00:07:34.430 --> 00:07:37.300
So, there's a lot of things
going on at Khan Academy,

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:38.986
some of which we have talked
about and some of which

00:07:38.986 --> 00:07:43.840
we're trying to accelerate
because of this COVID crisis.

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:45.950
English and language arts,
many of y'all are familiar,

00:07:45.950 --> 00:07:48.480
we just launched what's
known as a beta version.

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:52.730
In software world beta is
like, it's not even quite

00:07:52.730 --> 00:07:54.560
a first version, it's
a version that you put

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:57.070
out there to start to
learn about how it's used,

00:07:57.070 --> 00:07:59.216
what's good about it, what's not.

00:07:59.216 --> 00:08:01.799
We know it's not a perfect version.

00:08:01.799 --> 00:08:06.760
A lot of the modalities on
Khan Academy were first made

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:08.671
for the math and sciences
and so you can imagine

00:08:08.671 --> 00:08:10.709
when we try to put reading comprehension

00:08:10.709 --> 00:08:14.460
into that same modality sometimes
it's a little bit awkward.

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:18.760
Ideally in reading comprehension,
it's not just a passage

00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:21.100
and a question and then another
passage and the question,

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:23.100
you want a passage and
then four or five questions

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:24.410
like we do in our SAT practice.

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:27.890
So we're working on, hopefully
over the next many months,

00:08:27.890 --> 00:08:29.583
improving things like that.

00:08:29.583 --> 00:08:32.150
We are continuing to
partner with districts

00:08:32.150 --> 00:08:34.500
and we might be accelerating
that over the course

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:37.120
of this crisis because
more and more districts

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:40.090
are thinking about how do
they keep students learning

00:08:40.090 --> 00:08:43.490
in times of school closures,
and that's on top of the need

00:08:43.490 --> 00:08:45.150
that districts have always recognized.

00:08:45.150 --> 00:08:47.020
That they administer standardized tests,

00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:49.230
they see that students,
some students are ready

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:51.250
for grade level work, some
students have a lot of gaps

00:08:51.250 --> 00:08:53.540
in their knowledge, some
students are ready to move ahead.

00:08:53.540 --> 00:08:54.980
But they've historically not had

00:08:54.980 --> 00:08:57.300
a good way of differentiating.

00:08:57.300 --> 00:08:59.650
And tools like Khan Academy
can be helpful there.

00:08:59.650 --> 00:09:01.320
So we're working with
districts to provide things

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:04.193
like district dashboards
and rostering with them.

00:09:05.080 --> 00:09:07.530
We hope to continue to, you'll be seeing

00:09:07.530 --> 00:09:09.940
our chemistry content
is getting significantly

00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:12.260
flushed out, we are constantly improving

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:16.180
our math content, we are hoping
to add even more subjects.

00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:18.830
So we're talking to philanthropists about

00:09:18.830 --> 00:09:21.390
wouldn't it be great if we
could fund middle school science

00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:22.640
and things like that.

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:24.316
And so that's, if you're a philanthropist,

00:09:24.316 --> 00:09:27.090
please contact us if you're interested

00:09:27.090 --> 00:09:28.739
in funding things like that.

00:09:28.739 --> 00:09:32.310
Longer term, we're
thinking about simple ways

00:09:32.310 --> 00:09:35.250
to help place and diagnose
folks on Khan Academy.

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:37.250
So right now we have
a lot of the resources

00:09:37.250 --> 00:09:40.300
that you can start as early as
pre-k with Khan Academy Kids.

00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:44.370
You can see Cody right behind
me gazing over my shoulder.

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:46.340
But we have most of the content from pre-k

00:09:46.340 --> 00:09:48.700
all the way through elementary,
middle, and high school.

00:09:48.700 --> 00:09:50.960
For sure in math and
science, but increasingly

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:53.500
in the humanities and
English and language arts.

00:09:53.500 --> 00:09:55.657
But, because there's so much
content, sometimes folks

00:09:55.657 --> 00:09:57.427
are like, "Where do I start, what do I do

00:09:57.427 --> 00:09:59.437
"if maybe I'm in a certain
grade but I have gaps,

00:09:59.437 --> 00:10:01.377
"or maybe I'm in a certain
grade, I'm ready to move ahead,

00:10:01.377 --> 00:10:02.900
"or I already know some of the material."

00:10:02.900 --> 00:10:05.360
And we've been putting out
material during this crisis

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:07.480
about how to use our mastery mechanics.

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:09.160
If you think you already
know some of sixth grade,

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:12.360
go take the course challenge
and when you start seeing

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:14.850
diminishing returns there,
go take the unit test,

00:10:14.850 --> 00:10:15.683
et cetera et cetera.

00:10:15.683 --> 00:10:18.470
But we wanna make it
more, we wanna put that

00:10:18.470 --> 00:10:20.940
in the experience better,
make it a little bit easier

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:23.190
for people to understand where they can go

00:10:23.190 --> 00:10:25.760
and what types of courses
maybe we can curate

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:28.360
for certain, for those different cases.

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:30.910
So the simple answer is we
just add a lot of content,

00:10:30.910 --> 00:10:35.890
a lot more content, we wanna
make it a lot easier to use,

00:10:35.890 --> 00:10:38.350
find what you need to work
on, a lot more engaging.

00:10:38.350 --> 00:10:40.990
We have some, I hope we can
get this for back to school,

00:10:40.990 --> 00:10:43.260
but we definitely have some
team members who are thinking

00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:46.860
about how to make the navigation
on the site a lot easier.

00:10:46.860 --> 00:10:49.340
So we definitely have our
work cut out a lot there

00:10:49.340 --> 00:10:51.910
'cause we're literally
trying to cover 13 years

00:10:51.910 --> 00:10:53.870
of all of the core academic subjects

00:10:53.870 --> 00:10:58.360
and build tools for teachers
and support classrooms.

00:10:58.360 --> 00:11:00.630
Simultaneously, we have
59 translation projects

00:11:00.630 --> 00:11:01.870
around the world.

00:11:01.870 --> 00:11:03.900
So we are also thinking
about how we can support

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:06.290
other geographies, in
particular, places like Brazil

00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:08.400
and India where we
actually have, and Peru,

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:10.325
where we actually have team members there

00:11:10.325 --> 00:11:13.660
who are helping drive
Khan Academy's mission

00:11:13.660 --> 00:11:15.300
in those geographies.

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:17.193
I'm sure I'm missing things,
but that's some of what

00:11:17.193 --> 00:11:20.003
Khan Academy has going forward.

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:25.780
- Hi Sal, Adnan Abest
Kensuala from YouTube

00:11:25.780 --> 00:11:27.907
asks, "Sal, what's one
thing you wanna master

00:11:27.907 --> 00:11:29.007
"while being at home?"

00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:33.570
- One thing I wanna master
while being at home.

00:11:33.570 --> 00:11:38.570
I think I'm trying to master
balancing life and work.

00:11:38.610 --> 00:11:40.630
I don't know if that's,
you wanted a middle level

00:11:40.630 --> 00:11:42.177
answer like that, but you can imagine

00:11:42.177 --> 00:11:45.060
now with school closures,
my kids are home.

00:11:45.060 --> 00:11:48.190
And I've said this at other live streams,

00:11:48.190 --> 00:11:51.350
I think all of us who are,
especially in some degree

00:11:51.350 --> 00:11:53.630
my job at Khan Academy has gotten busier,

00:11:53.630 --> 00:11:55.680
there's more to do to
the previous question.

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:58.980
I feel more urgency
than ever to try to get

00:11:58.980 --> 00:12:01.550
some of this stuff done that
we've been talking about

00:12:01.550 --> 00:12:02.970
for a very long time.

00:12:02.970 --> 00:12:07.400
But if your five-year-old is screaming

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:10.580
or wants your attention,
it's a tough tension.

00:12:10.580 --> 00:12:13.010
And I don't feel sorry
for myself 'cause I know

00:12:13.010 --> 00:12:15.360
there's millions of
parents around the world

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:16.930
that are going through at
least what I'm going through,

00:12:16.930 --> 00:12:17.960
probably far worse.

00:12:17.960 --> 00:12:20.470
I have a lot of supports at home, my wife,

00:12:20.470 --> 00:12:23.390
my mother-in-law, we've all been trying

00:12:23.390 --> 00:12:25.968
to tag team this, and
frankly they've been,

00:12:25.968 --> 00:12:29.040
I've been able to lean on them even more

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:31.036
even though my wife has
a full time job as well.

00:12:31.036 --> 00:12:34.160
But because of all the Khan
Academy thing picking up.

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:37.330
So I think mastering
that balance is something

00:12:37.330 --> 00:12:40.560
that a lot of parents are
struggling with right now.

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:42.770
And one thing that's
clicked in my head recently

00:12:42.770 --> 00:12:46.240
is, you know, as much urgency
as I feel towards my job

00:12:46.240 --> 00:12:48.893
and as much urgency as
I feel towards work,

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:52.320
those are moments you
don't wanna let go of.

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:55.140
When your eight-year-old,
when she asks for help

00:12:55.140 --> 00:12:57.930
or she wants to show
something that she made

00:12:57.930 --> 00:13:01.160
or she has a question for
you, because that time,

00:13:01.160 --> 00:13:02.780
their childhood, goes by very fast.

00:13:02.780 --> 00:13:05.926
So I'm trying to just make
sure that I can enjoy that

00:13:05.926 --> 00:13:09.720
and give them the time they
need while also making sure

00:13:09.720 --> 00:13:13.460
that Khan Academy is able
to continue to support

00:13:13.460 --> 00:13:16.180
many, many, many people right now.

00:13:16.180 --> 00:13:18.958
That's, yeah, I think
that's the biggest thing.

00:13:18.958 --> 00:13:21.393
I've had a lifelong quest to,

00:13:23.530 --> 00:13:25.640
I guess you'd call it a fitness quest.

00:13:25.640 --> 00:13:29.750
It's a lot of things on a
diet and nutritional side

00:13:29.750 --> 00:13:32.100
that I've been trying to master,

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:34.063
but sometimes I fall off the wagon.

00:13:35.040 --> 00:13:38.687
So from YouTube, Mac Par asks,

00:13:38.687 --> 00:13:43.030
"Did you expect Khan Academy
to be this successful?"

00:13:43.030 --> 00:13:44.913
Mac, yes and no.

00:13:46.900 --> 00:13:49.100
When I was a kid, and maybe
not even so much a kid,

00:13:49.100 --> 00:13:52.420
even when I was in my 20s and Khan Academy

00:13:52.420 --> 00:13:54.620
was a, I was tutoring my cousins,

00:13:54.620 --> 00:13:57.700
there was definitely a part
of me, and it's still there,

00:13:57.700 --> 00:14:00.600
that I daydream a lot and
I definitely would have

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:01.433
these moments where I'm like,

00:14:01.433 --> 00:14:02.897
"Hey, this thing that
I'm tutoring my cousins

00:14:02.897 --> 00:14:05.287
"and if I make tools for
them, maybe it scales

00:14:05.287 --> 00:14:06.807
"and if it's useful for
them, maybe it could be

00:14:06.807 --> 00:14:09.387
"useful for millions or
billions of people one day.

00:14:09.387 --> 00:14:12.717
"And wouldn't it be cool if
one day there was a new type

00:14:12.717 --> 00:14:14.647
"of institution where anyone on the planet

00:14:14.647 --> 00:14:15.950
"had access to learning."

00:14:15.950 --> 00:14:19.470
And I've cited being inspired by books

00:14:19.470 --> 00:14:21.500
like Isaac Asimov's "Foundations" series

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:23.840
where in that book a galactic civilization

00:14:23.840 --> 00:14:26.800
and someone creates a
foundation at the edge

00:14:26.800 --> 00:14:30.860
of the empire to shorten
a thousands of year

00:14:30.860 --> 00:14:32.590
galactic dark ages.

00:14:32.590 --> 00:14:34.380
And I remember always
being inspired by saying,

00:14:34.380 --> 00:14:39.350
yeah, knowledge is the thing
that can most support society

00:14:39.350 --> 00:14:44.350
and keep us from, I guess,
falling apart and devolving,

00:14:45.420 --> 00:14:46.720
so to speak.

00:14:46.720 --> 00:14:48.420
And so I've always thought those kind of,

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:50.470
I guess you could say, grand thoughts,

00:14:50.470 --> 00:14:52.270
but then usually after
I let myself go on one

00:14:52.270 --> 00:14:55.430
of those visions for five or 10 minutes,

00:14:55.430 --> 00:14:56.987
I'd be like, "Sal, be realistic.

00:14:56.987 --> 00:14:58.377
"Just put one foot in front of the other,

00:14:58.377 --> 00:15:01.740
"just do the next right
thing," to quote "Frozen II"

00:15:01.740 --> 00:15:02.990
and move from there.

00:15:02.990 --> 00:15:06.410
And so I would kind of then
just get very, very practical.

00:15:06.410 --> 00:15:08.920
I could have never imagined
that when I was tutoring

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:11.500
my cousins, 13, 14
years ago or when I quit

00:15:11.500 --> 00:15:13.970
my day job about 10 or 11 years ago

00:15:13.970 --> 00:15:17.404
that I would have truly
evolved as fast as it has.

00:15:17.404 --> 00:15:19.866
But I always did keep
that somewhat delusional

00:15:19.866 --> 00:15:21.940
thing in the back of my mind of well maybe

00:15:21.940 --> 00:15:25.790
this could be like a Smithsonian
or a great university

00:15:25.790 --> 00:15:28.970
or even operate at a
scale 100 or 1000 times

00:15:28.970 --> 00:15:30.450
larger than that.

00:15:30.450 --> 00:15:34.370
And now that Khan Academy
is of a larger scale

00:15:34.370 --> 00:15:38.370
and with this COVID crisis,
it is playing a, I guess

00:15:38.370 --> 00:15:40.170
you could say, an institutional role here

00:15:40.170 --> 00:15:43.720
for millions of folks,
tens of millions of folks

00:15:43.720 --> 00:15:45.970
around the world, I'm
a little less bashful

00:15:45.970 --> 00:15:48.210
speaking openly about these aspirations

00:15:48.210 --> 00:15:51.850
of it becoming a
multi-generational institution

00:15:51.850 --> 00:15:55.110
that can empower billions
of folks over time.

00:15:55.110 --> 00:15:57.862
So I still try to balance, and I think

00:15:57.862 --> 00:15:59.940
that's the interesting thing about life,

00:15:59.940 --> 00:16:03.760
you're always trying to
balance putting one leg

00:16:03.760 --> 00:16:05.780
in front of the other and
not getting too far ahead

00:16:05.780 --> 00:16:10.440
of yourself and staying humble, frankly,

00:16:10.440 --> 00:16:14.594
with having big goals, big
visions, and not being afraid

00:16:14.594 --> 00:16:19.420
to use a baseball analogy,
swing for the fences.

00:16:19.420 --> 00:16:22.500
And so that's always been
my hope for Khan Academy.

00:16:22.500 --> 00:16:24.220
But you know, I gotta say, even today,

00:16:24.220 --> 00:16:27.054
as far as Khan Academy
has gone, I'm always,

00:16:27.054 --> 00:16:29.410
my stress level can
sometimes be pretty high

00:16:29.410 --> 00:16:31.060
'cause I'm like, we can't blow it now.

00:16:31.060 --> 00:16:34.190
We have to go so much further
to empower so many more.

00:16:34.190 --> 00:16:36.300
Dan, more questions.

00:16:36.300 --> 00:16:39.247
- Yeah, so from YouTube,
Ashtush Tawari asks,

00:16:39.247 --> 00:16:42.097
"Hi Sal, many people are trying
to start a YouTube channel

00:16:42.097 --> 00:16:44.047
"and make tutorials just like you,

00:16:44.047 --> 00:16:46.647
"especially during this
crisis, but not everybody

00:16:46.647 --> 00:16:47.647
"is Salman Khan.

00:16:47.647 --> 00:16:49.440
"What advice would you give for someone?"

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:50.950
Actually, this question, Sal, we also get

00:16:50.950 --> 00:16:53.300
from a lot of teachers
who are having to do

00:16:53.300 --> 00:16:56.190
remote teaching right now and
they would love your advice

00:16:56.190 --> 00:16:59.003
in terms of just how to present online.

00:17:00.680 --> 00:17:03.120
- You know, my number one
advice, a lot of times

00:17:03.120 --> 00:17:05.760
folks end up focusing on the optics

00:17:05.760 --> 00:17:09.200
and does it look professional versus

00:17:09.200 --> 00:17:12.310
I would say, the authenticity
and the human element of it.

00:17:12.310 --> 00:17:16.100
And my number one advice
to anyone making content,

00:17:16.100 --> 00:17:18.120
whether they're doing it kind
of in a Khan Academy style

00:17:18.120 --> 00:17:21.310
with a pen tablet or they're
just doing a video like this

00:17:21.310 --> 00:17:24.450
is be yourself, you laugh at yourself,

00:17:24.450 --> 00:17:26.500
don't be afraid to make mistakes,

00:17:26.500 --> 00:17:28.280
be as authentic as possible.

00:17:28.280 --> 00:17:32.710
Human beings can know when
someone else is not themselves,

00:17:32.710 --> 00:17:33.880
when they're not being authentic,

00:17:33.880 --> 00:17:35.040
when they're reading a script.

00:17:35.040 --> 00:17:37.010
And you might be reading a
script for a very good reason,

00:17:37.010 --> 00:17:38.630
you wanna get all your language precise

00:17:38.630 --> 00:17:42.540
et cetera, et cetera, but I
think there's a nice balance.

00:17:42.540 --> 00:17:44.440
You wanna have the right
vocabulary, you wanna get

00:17:44.440 --> 00:17:46.310
your language right, but
there's something powerful

00:17:46.310 --> 00:17:49.740
about the learner seeing
that you are working

00:17:49.740 --> 00:17:52.330
through them, you're truly
thinking through it with them.

00:17:52.330 --> 00:17:56.166
I think another point
of advice is to view,

00:17:56.166 --> 00:17:58.290
I've heard from some teachers it's hard

00:17:58.290 --> 00:18:02.110
to teach to the screen
'cause you might be used

00:18:02.110 --> 00:18:03.310
to other folks in the room.

00:18:03.310 --> 00:18:07.240
And this is where maybe my
day dreaming imagination

00:18:07.240 --> 00:18:08.860
has been an asset.

00:18:08.860 --> 00:18:12.850
I imagine when I make content,
I imagine that I'm talking

00:18:12.850 --> 00:18:17.146
to my cousins or my
cousins at a certain age

00:18:17.146 --> 00:18:19.150
or my family members.

00:18:19.150 --> 00:18:22.490
I think it creates a connection
and I think even though

00:18:22.490 --> 00:18:24.496
we are using technology
and we're going to be,

00:18:24.496 --> 00:18:27.030
you know, I'm making the
content now and it might be used

00:18:27.030 --> 00:18:29.123
by thousands of people at another time,

00:18:30.140 --> 00:18:32.470
they'll feel that connection in it.

00:18:32.470 --> 00:18:34.710
Another thing I would say
is try to really focus

00:18:34.710 --> 00:18:38.220
on the connections and concepts.

00:18:38.220 --> 00:18:39.810
Too many times, not just in math,

00:18:39.810 --> 00:18:42.700
but pretty much in every
subject, concepts are taught

00:18:42.700 --> 00:18:44.446
in isolation and even a little comment,

00:18:44.446 --> 00:18:46.240
a little connection to like, well yeah,

00:18:46.240 --> 00:18:48.030
this is why plastics are stretchy,

00:18:48.030 --> 00:18:50.890
after just did a video
on amorphous solids.

00:18:50.890 --> 00:18:52.370
So that's why it's front of mind for me.

00:18:52.370 --> 00:18:55.732
Or that's why, I mean,
actually one of the fun things

00:18:55.732 --> 00:18:57.620
that actually I didn't include in my video

00:18:57.620 --> 00:19:00.610
is why eggs become solid
when you heat it up

00:19:00.610 --> 00:19:02.550
and that's because the proteins denature

00:19:02.550 --> 00:19:05.440
and what would be these bound proteins,

00:19:05.440 --> 00:19:07.050
these globular proteins they become these

00:19:07.050 --> 00:19:09.820
kind of stringy things
and get all entangled

00:19:09.820 --> 00:19:11.855
with each other and so
that's why the egg white

00:19:11.855 --> 00:19:15.380
gets kind of, kind of
becomes an amorphous solid,

00:19:15.380 --> 00:19:16.370
so to speak.

00:19:16.370 --> 00:19:18.890
But yeah, if you can connect
things to the real world,

00:19:18.890 --> 00:19:20.980
if you can connect things
to what people knew before.

00:19:20.980 --> 00:19:23.797
In Khan Academy videos
you'll often hear me say,

00:19:23.797 --> 00:19:26.037
"Hey, this might look
like another concept,

00:19:26.037 --> 00:19:27.587
"but it really is just an extension

00:19:27.587 --> 00:19:28.900
"of completing the square."

00:19:28.900 --> 00:19:32.440
The quadratic formula is
just completing the square

00:19:32.440 --> 00:19:36.750
for any quadratic or the
quotient rule in calculus

00:19:36.750 --> 00:19:41.110
really is just the product
and the chain rules combined.

00:19:41.110 --> 00:19:43.070
So I think the more that
you can connect things

00:19:43.070 --> 00:19:46.050
to what learners already
know, and there's a lot

00:19:46.050 --> 00:19:47.690
of learning science behind that as well.

00:19:47.690 --> 00:19:50.960
That people like to be grounded
in what they already know.

00:19:50.960 --> 00:19:52.590
And most of knowledge is that way,

00:19:52.590 --> 00:19:54.330
it is connected to other things.

00:19:54.330 --> 00:19:56.250
So that's' my best advice.

00:19:56.250 --> 00:19:57.370
And have fun doing it.

00:19:57.370 --> 00:19:59.780
If you don't have fun making the content,

00:19:59.780 --> 00:20:01.470
the person listening to the content

00:20:01.470 --> 00:20:02.840
is not going to have fun.

00:20:02.840 --> 00:20:04.732
And if you're not
passionate about the content

00:20:04.732 --> 00:20:06.780
and excited about it,
it's going to be hard

00:20:06.780 --> 00:20:08.880
for the other person
to be excited about it.

00:20:08.880 --> 00:20:11.080
So one thing I tell myself
and everyone at Khan Academy

00:20:11.080 --> 00:20:14.280
who makes content is, and not
just video content actually,

00:20:14.280 --> 00:20:16.449
even exercises and things like articles,

00:20:16.449 --> 00:20:18.870
if you're not excited
about it, if you don't feel

00:20:18.870 --> 00:20:20.390
the energy, if you don't see the beauty

00:20:20.390 --> 00:20:22.070
in the concept, it's going to be very hard

00:20:22.070 --> 00:20:23.810
for student to feel that same thing

00:20:23.810 --> 00:20:25.150
and we want them to feel that.

00:20:25.150 --> 00:20:28.100
And the reality is
pretty much every concept

00:20:28.100 --> 00:20:31.610
has a beauty to it, has
an intuitiveness to it,

00:20:31.610 --> 00:20:33.932
has a connection to other
things and has connection

00:20:33.932 --> 00:20:35.323
to the real world.

00:20:37.250 --> 00:20:39.360
So let's see, there's a
question, an interesting one

00:20:39.360 --> 00:20:41.417
from Angel Chobra from YouTube.

00:20:41.417 --> 00:20:44.047
"Are there any positives that resulted,

00:20:44.047 --> 00:20:47.177
"or maybe might result from
the coronavirus epidemic?"

00:20:48.130 --> 00:20:50.750
Well, as you can imagine as
we're in the midst of it,

00:20:50.750 --> 00:20:53.603
it's hard to see sometimes
the silver lining.

00:20:54.690 --> 00:20:57.280
If I were to, and it's easy
to point to a lot of things

00:20:57.280 --> 00:21:00.907
that are stressful or are not
so great about the crisis,

00:21:00.907 --> 00:21:03.440
if I were to say some of the positives

00:21:03.440 --> 00:21:06.340
or the silver linings, you
know, I talked a little earlier

00:21:06.340 --> 00:21:07.630
about spending time with family.

00:21:07.630 --> 00:21:10.050
I think a lot of us, if we're lucky enough

00:21:10.050 --> 00:21:12.900
to be with our families
right now, this is time

00:21:12.900 --> 00:21:17.900
that it's in every day
life when we're on our,

00:21:18.120 --> 00:21:20.060
doing the daily grind, so to speak,

00:21:20.060 --> 00:21:21.820
it's easy to take family for granted,

00:21:21.820 --> 00:21:23.500
it's easy to take friends for granted.

00:21:23.500 --> 00:21:26.460
And now we, whether it's in person,

00:21:26.460 --> 00:21:28.570
I'm able to spend more time with my kids,

00:21:28.570 --> 00:21:32.310
with my wife, this is
time we'll never forget

00:21:32.310 --> 00:21:33.360
and we'll never get back.

00:21:33.360 --> 00:21:35.860
So I think there's
something powerful there.

00:21:35.860 --> 00:21:37.990
I think this crisis,
there's something about it

00:21:37.990 --> 00:21:40.680
that makes us want to reach
out to our family members

00:21:40.680 --> 00:21:42.460
and be more connected with them.

00:21:42.460 --> 00:21:43.870
You know, I've talked on this live stream

00:21:43.870 --> 00:21:47.930
about my mom, she's alone,
lives alone in New Orleans

00:21:47.930 --> 00:21:52.030
right now which is having
a very high per capita

00:21:52.030 --> 00:21:54.499
incidence of COVID and my
mom, as you can imagine,

00:21:54.499 --> 00:21:57.080
is in that high risk group.

00:21:57.080 --> 00:21:59.830
And so just my worry
for her physical health,

00:21:59.830 --> 00:22:01.740
and frankly her mental
health, 'cause she's living

00:22:01.740 --> 00:22:03.857
by herself now, I've told myself, "Sal,

00:22:03.857 --> 00:22:05.967
"you can't just say hey,
I need to finish this task

00:22:05.967 --> 00:22:07.470
"at work and I'll call my mom tomorrow,"

00:22:07.470 --> 00:22:09.400
you've gotta call your mom every day

00:22:09.400 --> 00:22:11.620
and have a conversation with her.

00:22:11.620 --> 00:22:13.520
And it's been great, I've been doing it

00:22:13.520 --> 00:22:16.630
because of the crisis,
but I've kind of realized

00:22:16.630 --> 00:22:18.350
that I should be doing this all the time.

00:22:18.350 --> 00:22:20.530
That it shouldn't' just
be during the crisis.

00:22:20.530 --> 00:22:21.890
And we're having good conversations

00:22:21.890 --> 00:22:24.790
even though we're 2,000 miles apart.

00:22:24.790 --> 00:22:26.890
Better conversations,
frankly, then when I call

00:22:26.890 --> 00:22:29.530
every three days or we
only chat once a week.

00:22:29.530 --> 00:22:31.090
So I think that's been important.

00:22:31.090 --> 00:22:32.890
I've been connecting
with friends and family

00:22:32.890 --> 00:22:34.730
that I haven't talked
to for five, 10 years

00:22:34.730 --> 00:22:36.420
on Zoom and Google Hangouts.

00:22:36.420 --> 00:22:37.893
So those are positives.

00:22:38.905 --> 00:22:42.300
I think on the education side,
obviously school closures

00:22:42.300 --> 00:22:45.200
are very suboptimal, but I'm hoping

00:22:45.200 --> 00:22:48.380
that some of the tools
that we might accelerate,

00:22:48.380 --> 00:22:50.970
Khan Academy and others
might accelerate and build

00:22:50.970 --> 00:22:53.670
to respond to the crisis,
those will exist after

00:22:53.670 --> 00:22:55.100
so that's a silver lining.

00:22:55.100 --> 00:22:57.300
I'm hoping that some of the
habits, some of the patterns

00:22:57.300 --> 00:23:00.090
that students and teachers
and parents are forming

00:23:00.090 --> 00:23:03.120
during the crisis will
have lasting benefit.

00:23:03.120 --> 00:23:04.900
If you, as a student, are learning hey,

00:23:04.900 --> 00:23:07.650
I can structure my day,
I can keep learning,

00:23:07.650 --> 00:23:08.900
I can do that during the summers,

00:23:08.900 --> 00:23:10.228
I can do that when I'm an adult,

00:23:10.228 --> 00:23:13.000
even after I've graduated
from high school or college,

00:23:13.000 --> 00:23:14.430
those are super useful skills.

00:23:14.430 --> 00:23:17.511
Arguably the most important
skills that can last you

00:23:17.511 --> 00:23:19.460
an entire lifetime.

00:23:19.460 --> 00:23:21.580
We were just talking about
teachers and other folks

00:23:21.580 --> 00:23:24.487
making lessons online
to help their students,

00:23:24.487 --> 00:23:27.400
that's great, that's going
to have lasting implications

00:23:27.400 --> 00:23:28.840
beyond the crisis.

00:23:28.840 --> 00:23:31.650
Now that folks realize how
important online access

00:23:31.650 --> 00:23:34.820
at home is, we're seeing a
lot of telecom companies,

00:23:34.820 --> 00:23:38.220
philanthropists, Ray and Barbara Dalio,

00:23:38.220 --> 00:23:40.240
two philanthropists we know
who've been long time supporters

00:23:40.240 --> 00:23:42.450
of Khan Academy, they've
just distributed 60,000

00:23:42.450 --> 00:23:46.850
laptops in Connecticut to low
income high school students.

00:23:46.850 --> 00:23:49.550
We're seeing school
districts, like Las Vegas,

00:23:49.550 --> 00:23:51.700
Clark County, we had Dr. Jara on last week

00:23:51.700 --> 00:23:53.520
talking about how they
are distributing laptops

00:23:53.520 --> 00:23:55.520
and working with local cable companies

00:23:55.520 --> 00:23:58.620
to get wifi access or get online access.

00:23:58.620 --> 00:24:00.640
These are things that I
hope can lay groundwork

00:24:00.640 --> 00:24:04.524
that's permanent so that even
once we're past this crisis

00:24:04.524 --> 00:24:07.150
people will have that
access, which is empowering

00:24:07.150 --> 00:24:09.730
on so many levels.

00:24:09.730 --> 00:24:14.730
I think just the cracks that
we're seeing in our society,

00:24:14.840 --> 00:24:18.140
large numbers of people, due
to no fault of their own,

00:24:18.140 --> 00:24:21.200
aren't able to work now
because of they were working

00:24:21.200 --> 00:24:23.860
at a restaurant or a travel
agency or an airline,

00:24:23.860 --> 00:24:25.990
all of these things that even once we get

00:24:25.990 --> 00:24:28.290
through the worst of the
stay in place orders,

00:24:28.290 --> 00:24:30.270
it's unlikely that those industries

00:24:30.270 --> 00:24:31.260
are going to come back fully.

00:24:31.260 --> 00:24:33.710
We talked about that last
week with Bill Gates.

00:24:33.710 --> 00:24:35.849
I think that's making all of us recognize

00:24:35.849 --> 00:24:38.330
and governments recognize that hey,

00:24:38.330 --> 00:24:40.930
yeah, we probably do need
supports for these folks

00:24:40.930 --> 00:24:45.037
because for no fault of their own,

00:24:45.037 --> 00:24:48.530
in order to protect the public
safety, they're suffering

00:24:48.530 --> 00:24:52.300
and they need some way
to support their family.

00:24:52.300 --> 00:24:54.180
So regardless of where you
are on the political spectrum,

00:24:54.180 --> 00:24:56.250
I think it's making us
all realize that hey,

00:24:56.250 --> 00:24:59.170
there's some supports that folks need.

00:24:59.170 --> 00:25:01.040
So I think all of these types of things

00:25:01.040 --> 00:25:04.390
as we go through the
crisis will hopefully have

00:25:04.390 --> 00:25:07.220
some positive lasting implications.

00:25:07.220 --> 00:25:09.060
We could think of more,
feel free to add any

00:25:09.060 --> 00:25:11.470
that you could think of
on the message boards.

00:25:11.470 --> 00:25:13.120
We're happy to take a look at it.

00:25:14.110 --> 00:25:19.110
So from Facebook, Samara Abidrabo says,

00:25:20.897 --> 00:25:22.877
"For college students, do
you think that we should move

00:25:22.877 --> 00:25:24.267
"to more critical thinking tests

00:25:24.267 --> 00:25:27.997
"that are open book since
the works have changed

00:25:27.997 --> 00:25:30.187
"with so much access to information."

00:25:31.589 --> 00:25:32.930
Samara, I think yes and no.

00:25:32.930 --> 00:25:35.570
I think there's sometimes
a view, you'll hear this,

00:25:35.570 --> 00:25:37.730
and you'll actually hear this
commonly in education circles,

00:25:37.730 --> 00:25:40.480
oh people can Google everything now,

00:25:40.480 --> 00:25:44.020
why should people know, why
should people have facts

00:25:44.020 --> 00:25:47.130
or content knowledge that
they can Google that stuff

00:25:47.130 --> 00:25:49.420
and then they can just
work on the projects,

00:25:49.420 --> 00:25:52.580
or the applied, or the
critical thinking parts of it.

00:25:52.580 --> 00:25:54.030
Learning science actually tells us,

00:25:54.030 --> 00:25:56.560
I'm actually reading a book right now

00:25:56.560 --> 00:25:58.761
about this kind of a
survey of learning science,

00:25:58.761 --> 00:26:01.460
something that we take very
seriously at Khan Academy,

00:26:01.460 --> 00:26:05.500
and the reality is is
that factual knowledge,

00:26:05.500 --> 00:26:07.370
that content knowledge,
whether it's at a kind of

00:26:07.370 --> 00:26:09.720
a factual level or at a conceptual level,

00:26:09.720 --> 00:26:13.020
is super valuable for forming
that critical thinking.

00:26:13.020 --> 00:26:16.540
And so I think there's always
going to be a very important

00:26:16.540 --> 00:26:19.700
place for having that core knowledge

00:26:19.700 --> 00:26:22.450
and it will allow you to
build more connections.

00:26:22.450 --> 00:26:24.023
And if you have some
of that core knowledge,

00:26:24.023 --> 00:26:27.840
you don't have to apply as
much of your cognitive load

00:26:27.840 --> 00:26:31.650
when you have to do more complex
tasks through those things.

00:26:31.650 --> 00:26:33.820
One way to think about it is
you're playing basketball,

00:26:33.820 --> 00:26:35.130
or let's say you're learning a new sport.

00:26:35.130 --> 00:26:37.140
Let's say you've never
played basketball before,

00:26:37.140 --> 00:26:39.550
you can focus on the rules of the game

00:26:39.550 --> 00:26:43.410
and maybe movements that are
new to you, like dribbling,

00:26:43.410 --> 00:26:45.970
but you don't have to think
about things like walking

00:26:45.970 --> 00:26:48.360
or jumping, those are things
you know how to do already

00:26:48.360 --> 00:26:50.130
because they're so automatic.

00:26:50.130 --> 00:26:53.130
And there's analogs in
the learning world there.

00:26:53.130 --> 00:26:57.340
In math, something like your
core multiplication tables.

00:26:57.340 --> 00:26:58.590
It's of course, important to understand

00:26:58.590 --> 00:27:01.442
what multiplication really is
or how it can be represented,

00:27:01.442 --> 00:27:04.140
but if when you're doing
algebra you have to sit

00:27:04.140 --> 00:27:06.230
and think, wait, is nine times six,

00:27:06.230 --> 00:27:09.440
is it 54 or is it 56,
that's going to make it

00:27:09.440 --> 00:27:11.710
very hard for you to
engage in the algebra.

00:27:11.710 --> 00:27:14.690
Or if you are trying to think critically

00:27:14.690 --> 00:27:18.483
about economic, we talk
the situation right now,

00:27:18.483 --> 00:27:20.590
if you're trying to think about well,

00:27:20.590 --> 00:27:22.520
what could be the economic implications

00:27:22.520 --> 00:27:25.470
of this COVID crisis, if you don't already

00:27:25.470 --> 00:27:27.730
in the back of your mind
have context of saying

00:27:27.730 --> 00:27:30.130
okay, well we've gone through
this like this before,

00:27:30.130 --> 00:27:33.230
you had the Great Depression
which was in the 1930s,

00:27:33.230 --> 00:27:35.620
and then okay, what was the
historical context there,

00:27:35.620 --> 00:27:36.730
how did we get out of it?

00:27:36.730 --> 00:27:39.577
Well, there was World War
II, there was the Great Deal,

00:27:39.577 --> 00:27:42.470
sorry, the New Deal, and you could say,

00:27:42.470 --> 00:27:44.980
well how much did each of
those things contribute.

00:27:44.980 --> 00:27:47.290
If you don't have some of
that knowledge base there,

00:27:47.290 --> 00:27:51.190
it's very hard to think
critically about new situations.

00:27:51.190 --> 00:27:53.833
So I'm a big proponent and
a lot of what we try to do

00:27:53.833 --> 00:27:56.460
with Khan Academy is
give you that practice,

00:27:56.460 --> 00:28:00.210
not just at the most basic level of facts,

00:28:00.210 --> 00:28:02.620
but at the conceptual level
and at that understanding,

00:28:02.620 --> 00:28:05.370
comprehending level so
that you build that fluency

00:28:05.370 --> 00:28:07.700
so you can go higher and higher up that,

00:28:07.700 --> 00:28:11.320
you know, it's often known as
Bloom's Cognitive Taxonomy,

00:28:11.320 --> 00:28:15.000
Bloom's Cognitive Taxonomy of Skills

00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:17.350
so you can go higher and higher on that.

00:28:17.350 --> 00:28:19.150
So I don't think it's an either or,

00:28:19.150 --> 00:28:21.800
I think it's actually a value to having

00:28:21.800 --> 00:28:24.303
some of that core knowledge.

00:28:25.840 --> 00:28:30.537
So there's a question from
Patty Prentice on Facebook.

00:28:30.537 --> 00:28:32.367
"I am a college student, will the pandemic

00:28:32.367 --> 00:28:34.877
"affect availability of
classes offered in the future?

00:28:34.877 --> 00:28:37.447
"I heard that colleges
and universities will have

00:28:37.447 --> 00:28:39.890
"fewer students because of the pandemic."

00:28:39.890 --> 00:28:41.290
Patty, it's a fascinating question.

00:28:41.290 --> 00:28:43.810
I don't think, I definitely
am not an expert here

00:28:43.810 --> 00:28:46.700
and I have been talking to
some university administrators

00:28:46.700 --> 00:28:47.950
and people who are
familiar, and I don't think

00:28:47.950 --> 00:28:51.329
anyone really knows how
all of this will settle.

00:28:51.329 --> 00:28:54.810
As you could imagine, we hope
that we're through the worst

00:28:54.810 --> 00:28:58.270
of the stay in place
orders and school closures

00:28:58.270 --> 00:29:00.940
as we go back to school,
and hopefully the colleges

00:29:00.940 --> 00:29:02.950
and universities like K
through 12 will be able

00:29:02.950 --> 00:29:05.190
to open back up, but you could imagine

00:29:05.190 --> 00:29:08.530
that if the virus goes,
starts going exponential again

00:29:08.530 --> 00:29:09.500
and starts picking back up,

00:29:09.500 --> 00:29:10.720
it might have to be closed down again.

00:29:10.720 --> 00:29:12.750
So it is going to be a
hard time for universities.

00:29:12.750 --> 00:29:15.030
And there is a situation
where a lot of students

00:29:15.030 --> 00:29:16.990
are saying, hey well, the
university's going virtual,

00:29:16.990 --> 00:29:18.710
maybe I can just stay in virtual.

00:29:18.710 --> 00:29:21.330
So some of that might
happen and there are things

00:29:21.330 --> 00:29:24.180
like virtual universities and
MOOCs and things like that.

00:29:24.180 --> 00:29:26.760
You know, personally,
I found a lot of value

00:29:26.760 --> 00:29:30.360
in the physical experience of university,

00:29:30.360 --> 00:29:32.170
that's where I met my
wife, that's where I met

00:29:32.170 --> 00:29:34.920
some of my best friends
that I talked about

00:29:34.920 --> 00:29:37.100
that I connect with on a regular basis.

00:29:37.100 --> 00:29:39.680
Some of whom even start
Khan Academy and helped

00:29:39.680 --> 00:29:42.750
other things, so I think
college is really powerful

00:29:42.750 --> 00:29:46.643
from a social connection
and building yourself

00:29:46.643 --> 00:29:49.500
in many dimension point of view.

00:29:49.500 --> 00:29:52.850
But I think this crisis
does make us ask questions

00:29:52.850 --> 00:29:55.120
of like well, what
aspects are nice to have,

00:29:55.120 --> 00:29:56.980
what aspects are must haves,

00:29:56.980 --> 00:29:58.590
and there's a resourcing question.

00:29:58.590 --> 00:30:02.030
College is increasingly expensive
and so are there pathways

00:30:02.030 --> 00:30:04.490
that could be more
flexible, that can focus

00:30:04.490 --> 00:30:07.830
on the must haves, and
that could leave students

00:30:07.830 --> 00:30:10.120
with a lot less debt.

00:30:10.120 --> 00:30:12.080
When we say we want,
we're a not for profit

00:30:12.080 --> 00:30:14.550
with a free world class
education for anyone, anywhere,

00:30:14.550 --> 00:30:16.690
that's because we don't
think someone's education

00:30:16.690 --> 00:30:19.440
should hold back their ability
to be empowered in the world,

00:30:19.440 --> 00:30:21.620
but we know far too many
people, and I know people

00:30:21.620 --> 00:30:26.620
even in my own family who
the debt they've incurred

00:30:26.629 --> 00:30:29.590
has been somewhat debilitating, actually,

00:30:29.590 --> 00:30:31.820
for their ability to
participate in society

00:30:31.820 --> 00:30:32.653
because of college.

00:30:32.653 --> 00:30:35.520
So we'll see how all of this plays out.

00:30:35.520 --> 00:30:37.780
Well anyway, these were great questions,

00:30:37.780 --> 00:30:40.050
we will continue this.

00:30:40.050 --> 00:30:42.780
We're here every day at 12 p.m. Pacific,

00:30:42.780 --> 00:30:44.150
three p.m. Eastern in the U.S..

00:30:44.150 --> 00:30:47.100
So whatever time zone you all
are in you can figure it out,

00:30:47.100 --> 00:30:48.830
it's a place for us to
just stay connected,

00:30:48.830 --> 00:30:52.190
answer questions about
education, the economy,

00:30:52.190 --> 00:30:55.060
viruses, we have exciting slate of guests.

00:30:55.060 --> 00:30:57.200
We're probably gonna have
two or three guests a week

00:30:57.200 --> 00:30:59.360
that are interesting and have expertise

00:30:59.360 --> 00:31:00.220
in different areas.

00:31:00.220 --> 00:31:02.310
And I'll just remind everyone, stay safe,

00:31:02.310 --> 00:31:04.890
stay healthy, and if you're
in a position to do so,

00:31:04.890 --> 00:31:06.660
please think about
donating to Khan Academy,

00:31:06.660 --> 00:31:08.570
we need the help, especially if we're able

00:31:08.570 --> 00:31:11.470
to support more folks through this crisis.

00:31:11.470 --> 00:31:12.303
Thank you.

